                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "eff09e42-01e2-39bd-8649-8bcf724af25b",
                        "entry_id": "c2d30957-d957-315a-9163-9c581d2d46d4",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Often in the term meet one's Waterloo: a notable and decisive defeat following an encounter with a powerful opponent or a problem that is too difficult."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "5b104d1c-2a3e-4ada-a943-9365d3f05a48",
                                "sense_id": "eff09e42-01e2-39bd-8649-8bcf724af25b",
                                "text": "He flung his dime at a newsboy, got his Express, propped his back against the truck, and was at once rapt in the account of his Waterloo as expanded by the ingenious press."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9f7344f8-d9f4-4962-b5a3-22e33cf2236e",
                                "sense_id": "eff09e42-01e2-39bd-8649-8bcf724af25b",
                                "text": "Surprisingly, Greenfield did not meet his Waterloo in this showdown with his competitors."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "babd0ea4-155f-4ad3-9d7d-a1ccb239b628",
                                "sense_id": "eff09e42-01e2-39bd-8649-8bcf724af25b",
                                "text": "The skull ended up as Dubois’ personal Waterloo. His work received nothing but rejection and derision throughout the next decade, and the bones became dark secrets for much of the early twentieth century, locked away in Dubois’ home, unavailable for study by other scholars."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
